[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m There are **no runtime options, modes, or flags** — the public header declares a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m single function taking a single struct pointer, and there is no global state, no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m setter, no context object, and no compile-time `#ifdef`. So the configuration
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m surface is driven entirely by the *input shape* carried in `cp_image_t`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Axes the C code actually distinguishes:
